          "h": "Cost Breakdown: Total Investment Required",
          "body": "**MBA costs (2-year full-time, top US school)**:\n- **Tuition**: $100k–$150k\n- **Living expenses**: $30k–$50k (housing, food, travel; varies by location)\n- **Total direct cost**: $130k–$200k\n- **Opportunity cost**: 2 years not earning salary (~$150k–$300k, depending on your current job)\n- **Total cost of ownership**: $280k–$500k\n\n**MBA costs by school tier**:\n- **Top 10 (HBS, Wharton, Stanford, etc.)**: $140k–$160k tuition, $250k–$350k total cost\n- **Top 25 (Michigan Ross, Duke Fuqua, etc.)**: $100k–$130k tuition, $200k–$280k total cost\n- **Top 50 (state schools, regional programs)**: $60k–$100k tuition, $150k–$250k total cost\n- **Part-time/Online MBA**: $40k–$100k tuition, $50k–$120k total cost (less opportunity cost, but lower ROI)\n\n**MS costs (1.5–2 year, top school by field)**:\n- **Tuition**: $40k–$80k\n- **Living expenses**: $20k–$40k\n- **Total direct cost**: $60k–$120k\n- **Opportunity cost**: 1.5–2 years (~$80k–$200k, depending on current job)\n- **Total cost of ownership**: $140k–$320k\n\n**MS costs by field**:\n- **MS in CS (MIT, Stanford, CMU)**: $60k–$80k tuition, $100k–$150k total cost\n- **MS in Finance**: $50k–$80k tuition, $100k–$150k total cost\n- **MS in Engineering**: $40k–$70k tuition, $80k–$130k total cost\n- **MS in Science/Math**: $30k–$60k tuition, $70k–$120k total cost\n\n**Cost comparison**:\n- **MBA is 2–3x more expensive** than MS in total cost of ownership\n- **MBA is justified if** your salary jump is >$100k (consulting/finance do this)\n- **MS is justified if** you're staying in a technical role or pivoting to CS/finance (ROI is still strong but lower than MBA)\n\n**Scholarships & Funding**:\n- **MBA**: Merit-based scholarships common (top schools give 20–40% of students scholarships, avg $20k–$50k). Many work while doing part-time MBA.\n- **MS**: Merit-based scholarships common (varies by field; CS gets fewer scholarships, engineering gets more). Teaching/research assistantships reduce cost to $0–$20k.\n\n**Financing options**:\n- **Educational loans**: Both degrees have ~5–7% interest rates in India; 3–6% in US (federal loans). 10-year repayment typical.\n- **Employer sponsorship**: Some employers sponsor MBA (ask HR). Rare for MS (except tech companies for internal mobility).",
